{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"solving quadratics by factoring solving quadratics by factoring","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","fileName":"Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Solving Quadratics by Factoring","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Quadratic Equations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Zero Product Property","Factorable Quadratic","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"solving quadratics by factoring solving quadratics by factoring","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","fileName":"Solving Quadratics by Factoring.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Solving Quadratics by Factoring","heading":"Solving Quadratics by Factoring","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Solving a quadratic by factoring first rewrites the equation in standard form, factors the polynomial, and applies the [[Zero Product Property]]. For example,\n\n$$x^2+2x-3=0$$\n\nfactors as ((x+3)(x-1)=0), so (x=-3) or (x=1).\n\nThe method is efficient for a [[Factorable Quadratic]], but it depends on setting the product equal to zero. Factoring an expression without first moving all terms to one side does not directly create the separate linear equations.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Quadratic Equations","Zero Product Property","Factorable Quadratic","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":